1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,000
Roster Studio | A real, database-backed operations planning workspace.

2
00:00:12,005 --> 00:00:32,005
Try demo creates an isolated PostgreSQL workspace with 30 fictional employees.

3
00:00:32,007 --> 00:00:48,007
Calendars are month-aware: February 2028 includes leap day.

4
00:00:48,009 --> 00:01:04,009
Brand view distinguishes operating calendars from employee schedules.

5
00:01:04,009 --> 00:01:18,009
Person view shows working days, time off and individual allocation.

6
00:01:18,012 --> 00:01:30,012
Pod view groups resources under operational leads.

7
00:01:30,014 --> 00:01:46,014
Process view includes bundled services that cover that process.

8
00:01:46,016 --> 00:02:04,016
Eligibility is explicit. The scheduler never invents authorization.

9
00:02:04,020 --> 00:02:22,020
Services expose operating calendars, staffing, timed shifts and timezone.

10
00:02:22,024 --> 00:02:38,024
Catalog management supports people, brands, processes, pods and leads.

11
00:02:38,027 --> 00:02:54,027
Simulate a real sole-primary absence after generation.

12
00:02:54,030 --> 00:03:12,030
Coverage is revalidated immediately. Mandatory gaps block publication.

13
00:03:12,036 --> 00:03:30,036
The assignment inspector explains eligibility and available replacements.

14
00:03:30,044 --> 00:03:44,044
Humans authorize changes; automatic repair uses eligible resources only.

15
00:03:44,049 --> 00:03:56,049
Assessment demo | Server persistence, validated changes, explicit limitations.
